  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2004-p15.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Write \\(\\dfrac{x + y}{x} = 1 + \\dfrac{y}{x}.\\) Here \\(\\dfrac{y}{x} \\lt 0,\\) so the expression is largest when \\(\\left|\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right|\\) is smallest.\n\nThat happens with \\(y = 2\\) and \\(x = -4,\\) giving \\[ 1 + \\dfrac{2}{-4} = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}. \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "Rewrite \\(\\dfrac{x + y}{x} = 1 + \\dfrac{y}{x}\\)",
        "Since \\(\\dfrac{y}{x} \\lt 0,\\) make its absolute value as small as possible"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
